Myth: In the age of beginnings, the world knew only harsh daylight and complete darkness. There was no gentle transition, no mercy between extremes.

A young shepherd named Kael tended his flocks at the boundary between light and shadow. He watched how his lambs feared the sudden plunge into night, how they trembled when dawn came too quickly. He wished for something softer, something kind.

One twilight, Kael gathered embers from his dying fire and placed them in a clay bowl. He climbed to where earth touched sky and spoke to the horizon itself.

"The world needs gentleness," he said. "Let there be a bridge between your extremes."

He scattered the embers skyward. But instead of falling, they began to dance—swirling, multiplying, painting the sky in shades of amber, rose, and violet. The embers became the first sunset, teaching darkness and light to meet gradually.

Moon and stars saw this beautiful compromise and joined the dance, offering their own soft glow to ease the night.

Now, twice each day, the sky remembers Kael's kindness. The sunset and sunrise remind us that even the greatest opposites can learn to meet gently, that transitions matter as much as destinations, and that sometimes the most beautiful things exist in the spaces between.
